import Mathlib

/-!
# A082615: Palindromes such that the GCD of any pair of successive terms is a palindromic prime.

Source: https://oeis.org/A082615
Credit: @Solfanbtc

A palindrome is a positive integer whose decimal digits read the same backwards. The sequence is
generated by `a(1) = 2` and `a(n+1)` = the least palindrome `> a(n)` such that
`gcd(a(n), a(n+1))` is a palindromic prime (Danny Rorabaugh's description; this reproduces every
term of %S, %T, %U, checked in Python). Starting instead from another palindrome `x` gives the
sequence `x, next(x), next(next(x)), …`, which stops if some term has no successor.

Conjecture (Danny Rorabaugh, Mar 2015): every sequence generated in this fashion, beginning with a
palindrome that has a palindromic prime factor, is infinite and eventually coincides with this
sequence. Since each term determines the next, "eventually coincides" means that some term of the
sequence from `x` is a term of the sequence from `2` (after which the two agree).

Status: the first half (infinite) was proved by Robert Israel (comment of Mar 24 2015): if a
palindromic prime `p` divides the palindrome `a(n)`, then `p (1 + 10^m)` is a larger palindrome
whose gcd with `a(n)` is `p` for suitable `m`. The second half (eventual coincidence) is open;
checked in Python for every starting palindrome below `20000` whose orbit stays among
palindromes below `10^7` (the others, such as `12821`, jump past that range).
-/

namespace OMA.S_oeis_a082615

/-- `n` is a palindrome (in base 10), `n ≥ 1`. -/
def IsPal (n : ℕ) : Prop := 0 < n ∧ (Nat.digits 10 n).reverse = Nat.digits 10 n

/-- The admissible successors of `x`. -/
def Succ (x : ℕ) : Set ℕ := {k : ℕ | x < k ∧ IsPal k ∧ IsPal (Nat.gcd x k) ∧ (Nat.gcd x k).Prime}

/-- The least admissible successor (meaningful only when `Succ x` is nonempty). -/
noncomputable def next (x : ℕ) : ℕ := sInf (Succ x)

theorem main : ∀ x : ℕ, IsPal x → (∃ p : ℕ, p.Prime ∧ IsPal p ∧ p ∣ x) →
    (∀ k : ℕ, (Succ (next^[k] x)).Nonempty) ∧ ∃ i j : ℕ, next^[i] x = next^[j] 2 := by
  sorry

end OMA.S_oeis_a082615
